-- Q7 [VSEPR & Molecular Geometry · hard · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans C] (g51)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'VSEPR & Molecular Geometry',
    $qt51$In \(\text{XeF}_2\) the central xenon holds five electron domains, three of which are lone pairs that occupy the equatorial sites of the trigonal bipyramidal electron-pair arrangement. Given that constraint on lone-pair placement, what is the molecular geometry of \(\text{XeF}_2\)?$qt51$,
    $op51$[{"label":"A","text":"Trigonal pyramidal"},{"label":"B","text":"Bent, with an \\(\\text{F}-\\text{Xe}-\\text{F}\\) angle near \\(120^\\circ\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"Linear, with an \\(\\text{F}-\\text{Xe}-\\text{F}\\) angle of \\(180^\\circ\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"T-shaped"}]$op51$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex51$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is C because, with the three lone pairs filling the three equatorial positions of the trigonal bipyramid, the two \(\text{Xe}-\text{F}\) bonding pairs are forced into the axial positions, which lie \(180^\circ\) apart and give a linear molecule. Xenon supplies eight valence electrons; two are used in the two \(\text{Xe}-\text{F}\) single bonds, leaving six electrons as three lone pairs, so the steric number is \(2 + 3 = 5\), an exception to the octet rule giving trigonal bipyramidal electron-pair geometry. VSEPR places lone pairs equatorially because that arrangement gives each lone pair only two close (\(90^\circ\)) neighbors instead of three, minimizing the strongest lone-pair repulsions; the remaining axial sites hold the two fluorines, so the molecular geometry described by the atom positions alone is linear. (Choice A) Trigonal pyramidal arises from a steric number of four with one lone pair (an \(\text{AX}_3\text{E}_1\) case such as \(\text{NH}_3\)); \(\text{XeF}_2\) has only two bonded atoms and a steric number of five, so it cannot be trigonal pyramidal. (Choice B) A bent geometry with an angle near \(120^\circ\) would result if the two fluorines occupied two equatorial sites and a single lone pair the third, but here three lone pairs claim all equatorial sites, pushing the fluorines axial rather than equatorial. (Choice D) A T-shape is the shape of an \(\text{AX}_3\text{E}_2\) species (such as \(\text{ClF}_3\)) with three bonded atoms and two equatorial lone pairs; \(\text{XeF}_2\) has only two bonded fluorines and three lone pairs, so it reduces past the T-shape to a linear arrangement. Skill 2$ex51$,
    'hard', '5B', $cc51$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc51$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a51A$applies the steric-number-4 one-lone-pair shape (AX3E) to a steric-number-5 species$a51A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a51B$places the bonding pairs equatorially and a lone pair axially, inverting the lone-pair-equatorial rule to give a ~120 degree bent shape$a51B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a51D$cites the AX3E2 T-shaped geometry of a neighboring interhalogen instead of the AX2E3 linear case$a51D$ FROM q;

-- Q8 [VSEPR & Molecular Geometry · medium · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans D] (g52)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'VSEPR & Molecular Geometry',
    $qt52$A chemist counts the electron-density regions on the central iodine of the tetrachloroiodate ion \(\text{ICl}_4^-\) and finds four iodine–chlorine bonds together with two nonbonding electron pairs on iodine. Treating this as an \(\text{AX}_4\text{E}_2\) arrangement, the molecular geometry that minimizes electron-pair repulsion is most likely$qt52$,
    $op52$[{"label":"A","text":"tetrahedral, with all four \\(\\text{I}{-}\\text{Cl}\\) bonds directed toward the corners of a tetrahedron at about \\(109.5^\\circ\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"seesaw, with the two lone pairs placed in axial positions of a trigonal bipyramid"},{"label":"C","text":"octahedral, counting all six electron-density regions as \\(\\text{I}{-}\\text{Cl}\\) bonds"},{"label":"D","text":"square planar, with the four chlorine atoms in one plane and the two lone pairs above and below it"}]$op52$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex52$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is D because six regions of electron density around a central atom adopt an octahedral electron-domain arrangement, and for an \(\text{AX}_4\text{E}_2\) species the two lone pairs occupy positions \(180^\circ\) apart (trans, one above and one below the plane) to minimize the stronger lone-pair–lone-pair repulsion, leaving the four \(\text{I}{-}\text{Cl}\) bonding pairs in a single plane. The resulting molecular geometry (the shape defined by atom positions only) is square planar with \(\text{Cl}{-}\text{I}{-}\text{Cl}\) angles of \(90^\circ\). (Choice A) Tetrahedral at \(109.5^\circ\) is the geometry for an \(\text{AX}_4\) center with no lone pairs and only four electron-density regions; \(\text{ICl}_4^-\) has six regions, so its bonding pairs cannot spread to \(109.5^\circ\). (Choice B) Seesaw arises from an \(\text{AX}_4\text{E}_1\) center with five electron-density regions in a trigonal bipyramid; this both miscounts the electron domains as five rather than six and places lone pairs incorrectly, since in any bipyramidal count the single lone pair favors an equatorial site, not an axial one. (Choice C) Treating all six regions as \(\text{I}{-}\text{Cl}\) bonds ignores the two lone pairs that the iodine electron count requires, converting the \(\text{AX}_4\text{E}_2\) case into an \(\text{AX}_6\) case and yielding the wrong octahedral molecular shape. Skill 2.$ex52$,
    'medium', '5B', $cc52$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc52$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
