-- Q9 [VSEPR & Molecular Geometry · medium · Skill 1 · general-chemistry · ans A] (g53)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'VSEPR & Molecular Geometry',
    $qt53$Bromine pentafluoride \(\left(\text{BrF}_5\right)\) has a central bromine atom surrounded by six regions of electron density: five \(\text{Br}-\text{F}\) bonding regions and one lone pair. Assuming the lone pair and the five bonding regions arrange to minimize repulsion, which molecular geometry is expected for \(\text{BrF}_5\)?$qt53$,
    $op53$[{"label":"A","text":"Square pyramidal"},{"label":"B","text":"Octahedral"},{"label":"C","text":"Trigonal bipyramidal"},{"label":"D","text":"Seesaw"}]$op53$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex53$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is A because six regions of electron density around a central atom adopt an octahedral electron-domain arrangement, and when one of those regions is a lone pair, the five bonding regions are left in a square pyramidal molecular geometry. In OpenStax VSEPR terms, an \(\text{AX}_5\text{E}_1\) species (five bonded atoms plus one lone pair) keeps the octahedral electron geometry but the molecular shape is named only from the positions of the bonded atoms, giving a square base of four fluorines with one fluorine at the apex above bromine. (Choice B) Octahedral names the electron-domain geometry produced by all six regions, but the molecular geometry must be assigned from the five bonded atoms only, so the lone pair removes one vertex and the shape is not octahedral. (Choice C) Trigonal bipyramidal is the molecular geometry for five total regions of electron density with no lone pair; it results from counting only the five bonding regions and omitting the lone pair, which undercounts the regions to five. (Choice D) Seesaw is the molecular geometry of an \(\text{AX}_4\text{E}_1\) species built from five regions (four bonding plus one lone pair), a related hypervalent shape but one with a different region count than the six present here. Skill 1.$ex53$,
    'medium', '5B', $cc53$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc53$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
