-- Q10 [Molecular Polarity · easy · Skill 1 · general-chemistry · ans B] (g54)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Molecular Polarity',
    $qt54$Carbon dioxide, \(\text{CO}_2\), has a linear O=C=O geometry in which each \(\text{C=O}\) bond is polar. Given this constraint, the net molecular dipole moment of \(\text{CO}_2\) is most accurately described as$qt54$,
    $op54$[{"label":"A","text":"nonzero, because each \\(\\text{C=O}\\) bond is individually polar."},{"label":"B","text":"zero, because the two opposed \\(\\text{C=O}\\) bond dipoles cancel."},{"label":"C","text":"nonzero, because the lone pairs on oxygen create an asymmetric charge distribution."},{"label":"D","text":"zero, because the \\(\\text{C}\\) and \\(\\text{O}\\) atoms have nearly equal electronegativities."}]$op54$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex54$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is B because the molecular dipole is the vector sum of the bond dipoles, and in a linear geometry the two \(\text{C=O}\) bond dipoles point in exactly opposite directions and are equal in magnitude, so they cancel to zero. Molecular polarity depends on both bond polarity and molecular geometry: although each \(\text{C=O}\) bond carries a dipole pointing from \(\text{C}\) toward the more electronegative \(\text{O}\), the \(180^\circ\) O=C=O arrangement places these two vectors antiparallel, giving a net dipole moment of zero. (Choice A) The presence of polar bonds is necessary but not sufficient for a net molecular dipole; the geometric arrangement of those bond dipoles must also be asymmetric, which is not the case for linear \(\text{CO}_2\). (Choice C) The terminal oxygens in \(\text{CO}_2\) do bear lone pairs, but they are symmetrically disposed about the central carbon and do not generate a net asymmetric charge distribution that would produce a molecular dipole. (Choice D) Carbon and oxygen differ appreciably in electronegativity, so each \(\text{C=O}\) bond is genuinely polar; the molecule is nonpolar because of geometric cancellation, not because the bonds are nonpolar. Skill 1$ex54$,
    'easy', '5B', $cc54$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc54$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
