-- Q11 [Molecular Polarity · medium · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans C] (g55)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Molecular Polarity',
    $qt55$In \(\text{BF}_3\), boron has no lone pairs and bonds to three fluorine atoms whose higher electronegativity makes each \(\text{B}-\text{F}\) bond polar. Given this electron arrangement, which property most accurately characterizes the molecule overall?$qt55$,
    $op55$[{"label":"A","text":"It is polar because the three \\(\\text{B}-\\text{F}\\) bond dipoles add to a net dipole pointing away from boron."},{"label":"B","text":"It is polar because boron is electron-deficient and carries a partial negative charge relative to fluorine."},{"label":"C","text":"It is nonpolar because the trigonal planar geometry places the three equal \\(\\text{B}-\\text{F}\\) bond dipoles at \\(120^\\circ\\), so they cancel."},{"label":"D","text":"It is nonpolar because the \\(\\text{B}-\\text{F}\\) bonds are essentially nonpolar covalent, leaving no dipoles to sum."}]$op55$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex55$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is C because the molecule's three equal \(\text{B}-\text{F}\) bond dipoles are arranged symmetrically at \(120^\circ\) in one plane, so their vector sum is zero. With three bonding pairs and no lone pairs on boron, \(\text{BF}_3\) adopts a trigonal planar geometry; each \(\text{B}-\text{F}\) bond is polar with the dipole pointing toward the more electronegative fluorine, but three identical vectors separated by \(120^\circ\) in a single plane cancel exactly, leaving zero net molecular dipole even though every bond is polar. (Choice A) Three equal coplanar dipoles separated by \(120^\circ\) sum to zero rather than reinforcing into a net dipole, so the symmetry produces a nonpolar molecule. (Choice B) Boron is the less electronegative atom and carries a partial positive charge while fluorine is partial negative, and in any case a uniform redistribution of charge does not by itself make a symmetric molecule polar. (Choice D) The \(\text{B}-\text{F}\) bonds are strongly polar because of the large electronegativity difference between boron and fluorine; the molecule is nonpolar from geometric cancellation of real dipoles, not from an absence of dipoles. Skill 2$ex55$,
    'medium', '5B', $cc55$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc55$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
