-- Q12 [Molecular Polarity · medium · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans D] (g56)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Molecular Polarity',
    $qt56$A chemist examining phosphorus trichloride \(\text{PCl}_3\) notes that the central phosphorus atom forms three \(\text{P-Cl}\) bonds and retains one lone pair, giving a trigonal-pyramidal arrangement of the four electron domains. Each \(\text{P-Cl}\) bond is individually polar. Based on this structure, which statement most accurately predicts the net molecular polarity of \(\text{PCl}_3\)?$qt56$,
    $op56$[{"label":"A","text":"It is nonpolar, because the trigonal-pyramidal arrangement directs the three \\(\\text{P-Cl}\\) bond dipoles so that they cancel one another exactly."},{"label":"B","text":"It is nonpolar, because the lone pair on phosphorus produces a dipole that exactly opposes and cancels the sum of the three \\(\\text{P-Cl}\\) bond dipoles."},{"label":"C","text":"It is nonpolar, because phosphorus and chlorine differ too little in electronegativity for the \\(\\text{P-Cl}\\) bonds to carry any appreciable dipole."},{"label":"D","text":"It is polar, because the asymmetric trigonal-pyramidal geometry and the lone pair prevent the three \\(\\text{P-Cl}\\) bond dipoles from canceling, leaving a net dipole."}]$op56$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex56$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is D because the overall polarity of a molecule is the vector sum of its individual bond dipoles, and that sum depends on molecular geometry. In \(\text{PCl}_3\) the central phosphorus has four electron domains—three bonding \(\text{P-Cl}\) pairs and one lone pair—so the bonding atoms adopt a trigonal-pyramidal shape rather than a symmetric trigonal-planar one. Because chlorine is more electronegative than phosphorus, each \(\text{P-Cl}\) bond is polar with the negative end toward chlorine. In the trigonal-pyramidal arrangement these three bond dipoles point downward toward the same side of the molecule and do not oppose one another symmetrically, so they fail to cancel; the lone pair adds to this asymmetry. The result is a nonzero net dipole moment directed along the axis through phosphorus, making \(\text{PCl}_3\) a polar molecule. (Choice A) Exact cancellation of three identical bond dipoles requires a symmetric, planar 120-degree arrangement (trigonal planar); the trigonal-pyramidal geometry forced by the lone pair is asymmetric, so the dipoles reinforce rather than cancel. (Choice B) The lone pair does not cancel the bond dipoles—it reinforces the asymmetry by occupying one domain and pushing the bonding pairs to one side, contributing to the net dipole rather than nullifying it. (Choice C) Chlorine (\(\approx 3.0\)) is substantially more electronegative than phosphorus (\(\approx 2.1\)), so each \(\text{P-Cl}\) bond carries a real dipole; the bonds are clearly polar, and the molecular polarity question hinges on geometry, not on a vanishing bond dipole. Skill 2$ex56$,
    'medium', '5B', $cc56$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc56$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
