-- Q13 [Molecular Polarity · hard · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans A] (g57)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Molecular Polarity',
    $qt57$Chloroform, \(\text{CHCl}_3\), adopts a tetrahedral arrangement around carbon yet carries a net molecular dipole, unlike \(\text{CCl}_4\). Which feature of \(\text{CHCl}_3\) accounts for its polarity?$qt57$,
    $op57$[{"label":"A","text":"The three \\(\\text{C}-\\text{Cl}\\) bond dipoles and the one \\(\\text{C}-\\text{H}\\) bond dipole are nonequivalent in magnitude, so they cannot vectorially cancel."},{"label":"B","text":"The lone pairs on the three chlorine atoms occupy a fourth tetrahedral position, distorting the geometry into a polar trigonal-pyramidal shape."},{"label":"C","text":"Replacing one chlorine with hydrogen lowers the symmetry to a bent geometry, and bent molecules are always polar."},{"label":"D","text":"The \\(\\text{C}-\\text{H}\\) bond is more polar than each \\(\\text{C}-\\text{Cl}\\) bond, leaving a net dipole pointed from carbon toward hydrogen."}]$op57$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex57$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is A because the four bonds radiating from carbon are not all the same, so their individual bond dipoles are unequal and fail to sum to zero. In \(\text{CCl}_4\) the four identical \(\text{C}-\text{Cl}\) dipoles point toward the four vertices of a tetrahedron and cancel exactly by symmetry, giving a nonpolar molecule. In \(\text{CHCl}_3\) one vertex is occupied by an \(\text{H}\) atom: the three \(\text{C}-\text{Cl}\) bonds are strongly polar (\(\text{Cl}\) is much more electronegative than \(\text{C}\), so each dipole points from \(\text{C}\) toward \(\text{Cl}\)), while the \(\text{C}-\text{H}\) bond is only weakly polar. Because these vectors differ in magnitude, the vector sum is nonzero and the molecule is polar, with the net dipole oriented along the \(\text{H}-\text{C}\) axis toward the chlorine-rich side. (Choice B) Although each \(\text{Cl}\) carries lone pairs, those lone pairs do not occupy a bonding position on carbon; the central-atom geometry stays tetrahedral because carbon has four bonding pairs and no lone pairs, so the geometry is not distorted to trigonal pyramidal. (Choice C) Swapping one \(\text{Cl}\) for \(\text{H}\) leaves four electron domains on carbon, so the shape remains tetrahedral, not bent, and "bent molecules are always polar" overstates a geometry rule rather than identifying the operative cause. (Choice D) The polarity assignment is reversed: \(\text{Cl}\) (electronegativity \(\approx 3.0\)) is far more electronegative than \(\text{H}\) (\(\approx 2.1\)) relative to \(\text{C}\) (\(\approx 2.5\)), so the \(\text{C}-\text{Cl}\) bonds are the more polar ones and the net dipole points away from \(\text{H}\) toward the \(\text{Cl}\) atoms. Skill 2$ex57$,
    'hard', '5B', $cc57$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc57$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
