-- Q14 [Molecular Polarity · hard · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans B] (g58)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Molecular Polarity',
    $qt58$Carbon disulfide \( \text{CS}_2 \) and carbonyl sulfide \( \text{OCS} \) are both linear triatomic molecules with the carbon atom in the center. Based on how their bond dipoles combine along the molecular axis, which molecule is expected to possess a net molecular dipole moment?$qt58$,
    $op58$[{"label":"A","text":"\\( \\text{CS}_2 \\), because the two \\( \\text{C}{=}\\text{S} \\) bonds are polar and a molecule containing polar bonds is necessarily polar."},{"label":"B","text":"\\( \\text{OCS} \\), because the \\( \\text{C}{=}\\text{O} \\) and \\( \\text{C}{=}\\text{S} \\) bond dipoles differ in magnitude and do not cancel along the axis."},{"label":"C","text":"\\( \\text{CS}_2 \\), because its bent geometry prevents the two \\( \\text{C}{=}\\text{S} \\) dipoles from pointing in opposite directions."},{"label":"D","text":"\\( \\text{OCS} \\), because oxygen and sulfur sit on opposite ends and their equal but opposite dipoles reinforce each other."}]$op58$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex58$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is B because \( \text{OCS} \) is linear (O=C=S) yet asymmetric: the \( \text{C}{=}\text{O} \) bond dipole and the \( \text{C}{=}\text{S} \) bond dipole point along the same axis but differ in magnitude because oxygen is considerably more electronegative than sulfur. Since the two opposing vectors are unequal, they do not cancel, and a net dipole moment remains directed toward the oxygen end. By contrast, \( \text{CS}_2 \) (S=C=S) is also linear but symmetric: its two identical \( \text{C}{=}\text{S} \) bond dipoles are equal in magnitude and point in exactly opposite directions, so they cancel and the molecule is nonpolar. Molecular polarity is the vector sum of bond dipoles together with molecular shape, as developed in OpenStax Chemistry, so a molecule with polar bonds can still be nonpolar when symmetry makes the dipoles cancel. (Choice A) is incorrect because possessing polar bonds is not sufficient for molecular polarity; in symmetric \( \text{CS}_2 \) the two polar \( \text{C}{=}\text{S} \) dipoles cancel exactly, leaving a nonpolar molecule. (Choice C) is incorrect because \( \text{CS}_2 \) is not bent; with two regions of electron density and no lone pairs on carbon it is linear, and that linear symmetry is precisely what allows the equal \( \text{C}{=}\text{S} \) dipoles to oppose and cancel. (Choice D) names the correct molecule but for a reversed reason: the oxygen and sulfur dipoles do not reinforce, and they are not equal; their unequal opposing magnitudes are exactly why a residual net dipole survives. Skill 2$ex58$,
    'hard', '5B', $cc58$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc58$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
