-- Q15 [Molecular Polarity · medium · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans C] (g59)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Molecular Polarity',
    $qt59$In \(\text{SF}_6\), the central sulfur atom forms six equivalent polar \(\text{S-F}\) bonds with no lone pairs on sulfur. Given this constraint, what is the net molecular dipole moment of \(\text{SF}_6\)?$qt59$,
    $op59$[{"label":"A","text":"Nonzero, because each \\(\\text{S-F}\\) bond is polar and six polar bonds reinforce one another."},{"label":"B","text":"Nonzero, because sulfur exceeds the octet and the expanded valence shell creates an asymmetric charge distribution."},{"label":"C","text":"Zero, because the six identical bond dipoles are arranged octahedrally and their vector sum cancels."},{"label":"D","text":"Zero, because fluorine and sulfur have nearly equal electronegativities, so the individual \\(\\text{S-F}\\) bonds are essentially nonpolar."}]$op59$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex59$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is C because the six equivalent \(\text{S-F}\) bond dipoles point toward the six vertices of an octahedron, and this symmetric arrangement makes the vector sum of the dipoles equal to zero. With six bonding regions and no lone pairs on sulfur, VSEPR predicts an octahedral geometry, in which each bond dipole is directly opposed by an identical bond dipole on the opposite side; opposing pairs cancel exactly, so the resultant (net) molecular dipole is zero and \(\text{SF}_6\) is nonpolar even though every \(\text{S-F}\) bond is individually polar. (Choice A) Polar bonds do not "reinforce" simply because there are many of them; molecular polarity depends on the vector sum over the geometry, and in the symmetric octahedral arrangement the bond dipoles oppose and cancel rather than add. (Choice B) Sulfur does expand its valence shell to accommodate six bonds, but exceeding the octet does not by itself create a net dipole; the determining factor is the symmetry of the bond-dipole arrangement, which here is fully symmetric. (Choice D) Sulfur (≈2.6) and fluorine (≈4.0) differ substantially in electronegativity, so the \(\text{S-F}\) bonds are strongly polar; the molecule is nonpolar because of dipole cancellation, not because the bonds lack polarity. Skill 2$ex59$,
    'medium', '5B', $cc59$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc59$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
