-- Q16 [Bond Polarity & Dipole Moment · easy · Skill 1 · general-chemistry · ans D] (g60)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Bond Polarity & Dipole Moment',
    $qt60$In the diatomic molecule \(\text{HF}\), fluorine is more electronegative than hydrogen. Which way does the bond dipole point along the \(\text{H}-\text{F}\) bond?$qt60$,
    $op60$[{"label":"A","text":"From \\(\\text{F}\\) toward \\(\\text{H}\\), because the smaller \\(\\text{H}\\) atom pulls the shared electrons closer to itself"},{"label":"B","text":"The bond has no net dipole, because both atoms share the bonding electrons equally"},{"label":"C","text":"From \\(\\text{F}\\) toward \\(\\text{H}\\), because the more electronegative atom carries the positive end of the dipole"},{"label":"D","text":"From \\(\\text{H}\\) toward \\(\\text{F}\\), because the more electronegative \\(\\text{F}\\) draws the shared electrons toward itself"}]$op60$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex60$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is D because the bond dipole points from the partially positive \(\text{H}\) end toward the partially negative \(\text{F}\) end, since the more electronegative \(\text{F}\) pulls the shared bonding pair toward itself. In a polar covalent bond the more electronegative atom acquires the partial negative charge (\(\delta-\)) and the less electronegative atom acquires the partial positive charge (\(\delta+\)); the bond dipole vector by convention points from the \(\delta+\) atom to the \(\delta-\) atom. Because \(\text{F}\) has the higher electronegativity, electron density is displaced toward \(\text{F}\), making \(\text{F}\) the \(\delta-\) end and \(\text{H}\) the \(\delta+\) end, so the dipole points from \(\text{H}\) to \(\text{F}\). (Choice A) Pointing from \(\text{F}\) toward \(\text{H}\) reverses the displacement of electron density; atomic size does not override electronegativity here, and the shared electrons are drawn toward \(\text{F}\), not \(\text{H}\). (Choice B) Equal sharing applies only to a bond between identical atoms with equal electronegativity, whereas \(\text{H}\) and \(\text{F}\) differ substantially in electronegativity, so the bond is polar and does carry a net dipole. (Choice C) The direction is reversed: the more electronegative atom bears the \(\delta-\) (negative) end of the dipole, not the positive end, so the dipole cannot point away from \(\text{F}\) on the basis stated. Skill 1$ex60$,
    'easy', '5B', $cc60$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc60$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
