-- Q17 [Bond Polarity & Dipole Moment · easy · Skill 1 · general-chemistry · ans A] (g61)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Bond Polarity & Dipole Moment',
    $qt61$In methanol, \(\text{CH}_3\text{OH}\), the carbon and oxygen are directly bonded. For this isolated \(\text{C}-\text{O}\) bond, the partial negative charge resides on which atom, and toward which atom does the bond dipole point?$qt61$,
    $op61$[{"label":"A","text":"The partial negative charge resides on \\(\\text{O}\\), and the bond dipole points from \\(\\text{C}\\) toward \\(\\text{O}\\)."},{"label":"B","text":"The partial negative charge resides on \\(\\text{C}\\), and the bond dipole points from \\(\\text{O}\\) toward \\(\\text{C}\\)."},{"label":"C","text":"The partial negative charge resides on \\(\\text{O}\\), and the bond dipole points from \\(\\text{O}\\) toward \\(\\text{C}\\)."},{"label":"D","text":"The partial negative charge is shared equally, so the \\(\\text{C}-\\text{O}\\) bond carries no net dipole."}]$op61$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex61$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is A because oxygen is more electronegative than carbon, so it draws the shared bonding electrons toward itself, placing the partial negative charge (\(\delta^-\)) on \(\text{O}\) and directing the bond dipole from \(\text{C}\) toward \(\text{O}\). By convention the dipole arrow points from the partial positive end (\(\delta^+\), the less electronegative \(\text{C}\)) toward the partial negative end (\(\delta^-\), the more electronegative \(\text{O}\)); because the electronegativity difference \(\Delta\text{EN}\) between \(\text{C}\) and \(\text{O}\) is nonzero, the bond is polar and a net bond dipole exists. (Choice B) This reverses the polarity, incorrectly placing \(\delta^-\) on \(\text{C}\) and pointing the dipole toward \(\text{C}\), which would only hold if carbon were the more electronegative atom. (Choice C) This correctly locates \(\delta^-\) on \(\text{O}\) but draws the arrow toward \(\text{C}\); the dipole arrow must point toward the partial-negative atom, not away from it. (Choice D) A \(\text{C}-\text{O}\) bond joins atoms of unequal electronegativity, so the electrons are not shared equally and the bond is polar rather than dipole-free. Skill 1$ex61$,
    'easy', '5B', $cc61$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc61$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
