-- Q18 [Bond Polarity & Dipole Moment · medium · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans B] (g62)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Bond Polarity & Dipole Moment',
    $qt62$Using Pauling electronegativities \(\text{H}=2.20\), \(\text{C}=2.55\), \(\text{N}=3.04\), and \(\text{O}=3.44\), a chemist compares the single bonds \(\text{C}-\text{H}\), \(\text{N}-\text{H}\), and \(\text{O}-\text{H}\). Which bond is most polar?$qt62$,
    $op62$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{C}-\\text{H}\\), because its small \\(\\Delta\\text{EN}\\) of \\(0.35\\) concentrates the bonding electrons most sharply toward one atom"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{O}-\\text{H}\\), because its \\(\\Delta\\text{EN}\\) of \\(1.24\\) is the largest of the three"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{N}-\\text{H}\\), because its \\(\\Delta\\text{EN}\\) of \\(0.84\\) is large enough to make it the most polar of the set"},{"label":"D","text":"All three are equally polar, because each bond shares the identical \\(\\text{H}\\) atom as one partner"}]$op62$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex62$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is B because \(\text{O}-\text{H}\) has the largest electronegativity difference (\(\Delta\text{EN}=3.44-2.20=1.24\)), so its bonding electrons are pulled most strongly toward one atom, giving the greatest bond polarity. Bond polarity scales with the magnitude of \(\Delta\text{EN}\) between the two bonded atoms: a larger difference means a more unequal sharing of the bonding pair and a larger bond dipole. Computing each difference gives \(\text{C}-\text{H}=2.55-2.20=0.35\), \(\text{N}-\text{H}=3.04-2.20=0.84\), and \(\text{O}-\text{H}=3.44-2.20=1.24\), so \(\text{O}-\text{H}>\text{N}-\text{H}>\text{C}-\text{H}\) and \(\text{O}-\text{H}\) is most polar. (Choice A) \(\text{C}-\text{H}\) has the smallest \(\Delta\text{EN}\) of the set, so it is the least polar, not the most; greater polarity corresponds to a larger \(\Delta\text{EN}\), not a smaller one. (Choice C) \(\text{N}-\text{H}\) is genuinely polar and is intermediate in the series, but its \(\Delta\text{EN}\) of \(0.84\) is smaller than the \(1.24\) of \(\text{O}-\text{H}\), so it is not the most polar bond. (Choice D) The bonds are not equally polar, because polarity is governed by the difference in electronegativity between the two partners, and the second atom (\(\text{C}\), \(\text{N}\), or \(\text{O}\)) differs in each case even though \(\text{H}\) is common to all three. Skill 2$ex62$,
    'medium', '5B', $cc62$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc62$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
