-- Q19 [Bond Polarity & Dipole Moment · easy · Skill 1 · general-chemistry · ans C] (g63)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Bond Polarity & Dipole Moment',
    $qt63$Within an amino acid backbone, three single bonds connect carbon to its neighbors: \(\text{C}-\text{N}\), \(\text{C}-\text{O}\), and \(\text{C}-\text{C}\). Given the electronegativity values \(\text{C} = 2.5\), \(\text{N} = 3.0\), and \(\text{O} = 3.5\), which of these bonds is the least polar?$qt63$,
    $op63$[{"label":"A","text":"The \\(\\text{C}-\\text{N}\\) bond, because its electronegativity difference of \\(0.5\\) is the smallest nonzero value among the three."},{"label":"B","text":"The \\(\\text{C}-\\text{O}\\) bond, because oxygen's high electronegativity pulls electron density so strongly that the bond behaves as nonpolar."},{"label":"C","text":"The \\(\\text{C}-\\text{C}\\) bond, because its electronegativity difference of \\(0\\) leaves the bonding electrons shared equally."},{"label":"D","text":"The \\(\\text{C}-\\text{N}\\) bond, because nitrogen lies between carbon and oxygen and therefore cancels the bond dipole."}]$op63$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex63$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is C because the two bonded carbons have identical electronegativities, giving an electronegativity difference of \(0\) and a bond in which the shared electrons are distributed equally. Bond polarity scales with the absolute electronegativity difference \(\Delta\text{EN}\) between the two bonded atoms: the larger the \(\Delta\text{EN}\), the more the shared pair is displaced toward the more electronegative atom and the more polar the bond. Here \(\text{C}-\text{N}\) has \(\Delta\text{EN} = |2.5 - 3.0| = 0.5\) and \(\text{C}-\text{O}\) has \(\Delta\text{EN} = |2.5 - 3.5| = 1.0\), while \(\text{C}-\text{C}\) has \(\Delta\text{EN} = |2.5 - 2.5| = 0\); a bond between two atoms of the same element is purely covalent (nonpolar), so \(\text{C}-\text{C}\) is the least polar. (Choice A) The \(\text{C}-\text{N}\) bond has the smallest nonzero \(\Delta\text{EN}\) of \(0.5\), but it is still larger than the \(0\) difference of \(\text{C}-\text{C}\), so \(\text{C}-\text{N}\) is more polar, not the least polar. (Choice B) A large electronegativity difference such as the \(1.0\) for \(\text{C}-\text{O}\) makes a bond more polar, not nonpolar; strong unequal sharing produces the greatest partial charges and the largest bond dipole among the three. (Choice D) The polarity of a single \(\text{C}-\text{N}\) bond is set by the \(\Delta\text{EN}\) of its two atoms alone; an intermediate electronegativity for nitrogen relative to oxygen does not cancel that bond's dipole, and there is no separate \(\text{C}-\text{O}\) dipole acting along the \(\text{C}-\text{N}\) bond to oppose it. Skill 1$ex63$,
    'easy', '5B', $cc63$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc63$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
