-- Q20 [Bond Polarity & Dipole Moment · medium · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans D] (g64)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Bond Polarity & Dipole Moment',
    $qt64$In the gas-phase diatomics \(\text{H}-\text{Cl}\), \(\text{H}-\text{Br}\), and \(\text{H}-\text{I}\), the Pauling electronegativities are \(\text{H} = 2.1\), \(\text{Cl} = 3.0\), \(\text{Br} = 2.8\), and \(\text{I} = 2.5\). Ranked from largest to smallest bond-dipole magnitude, the three bonds most likely fall in which order?$qt64$,
    $op64$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{H}-\\text{I} > \\text{H}-\\text{Br} > \\text{H}-\\text{Cl}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{H}-\\text{Cl} > \\text{H}-\\text{I} > \\text{H}-\\text{Br}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{H}-\\text{Cl} \\approx \\text{H}-\\text{Br} \\approx \\text{H}-\\text{I}\\) (all essentially equal)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{H}-\\text{Cl} > \\text{H}-\\text{Br} > \\text{H}-\\text{I}\\)"}]$op64$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex64$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is D because the bond-dipole magnitude tracks the absolute electronegativity difference \(|\Delta\text{EN}|\) between the bonded atoms, and these differences fall as \(\text{H}-\text{Cl} (0.9) > \text{H}-\text{Br} (0.7) > \text{H}-\text{I} (0.4)\). For a two-atom bond the dipole points toward the more electronegative atom and grows as the partner atoms share the bonding electrons more unequally; here hydrogen is common to all three bonds, so the ranking is set entirely by how electronegative the halogen is relative to hydrogen. Computing \(|\Delta\text{EN}|\) gives \(|2.1-3.0| = 0.9\) for \(\text{H}-\text{Cl}\), \(|2.1-2.8| = 0.7\) for \(\text{H}-\text{Br}\), and \(|2.1-2.5| = 0.4\) for \(\text{H}-\text{I}\), so the bond dipole is largest for \(\text{H}-\text{Cl}\) and smallest for \(\text{H}-\text{I}\). (Choice A) This is the exact reverse ordering, which would follow from ranking by increasing halogen size or polarizability rather than by \(|\Delta\text{EN}|\); a larger, more diffuse halogen actually has a smaller electronegativity difference with hydrogen and therefore a smaller, not larger, bond dipole. (Choice B) This correctly identifies \(\text{H}-\text{Cl}\) as the largest but transposes the lower two, placing \(\text{H}-\text{I}\) above \(\text{H}-\text{Br}\); the differences \(0.7\) for \(\text{H}-\text{Br}\) and \(0.4\) for \(\text{H}-\text{I}\) show \(\text{H}-\text{Br}\) is the more polar of the pair. (Choice C) Treating the three bond dipoles as equal ignores that the halogen electronegativities differ \((3.0, 2.8, 2.5)\); because hydrogen is shared while the halogen varies, the \(|\Delta\text{EN}|\) values are distinct and the dipoles are not equal. Skill 2$ex64$,
    'medium', '5B', $cc64$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc64$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
