UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a64B$identifies the largest dipole correctly but transposes the two smaller halogens from a faulty difference comparison$a64B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a64C$assumes a shared hydrogen makes all H-halogen bond dipoles equal, ignoring the varying halogen electronegativity$a64C$ FROM q;

-- Q21 [Bond Polarity & Dipole Moment · hard · Skill 4 · general-chemistry · ans A] (g65)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Bond Polarity & Dipole Moment',
    $qt65$A chemist uses the Pauling electronegativity values \(\text{C}=2.55\), \(\text{F}=3.98\), \(\text{Cl}=3.16\), \(\text{Br}=2.96\), and \(\text{I}=2.66\) to compare the four isolated carbon-halogen bonds \(\text{C}-\text{F}\), \(\text{C}-\text{Cl}\), \(\text{C}-\text{Br}\), and \(\text{C}-\text{I}\). Ranking these bonds from largest to smallest bond-dipole magnitude gives which order?$qt65$,
    $op65$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{C}-\\text{F} > \\text{C}-\\text{Cl} > \\text{C}-\\text{Br} > \\text{C}-\\text{I}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{C}-\\text{I} > \\text{C}-\\text{Br} > \\text{C}-\\text{Cl} > \\text{C}-\\text{F}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{C}-\\text{Cl} > \\text{C}-\\text{F} > \\text{C}-\\text{Br} > \\text{C}-\\text{I}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{C}-\\text{F} > \\text{C}-\\text{I} > \\text{C}-\\text{Cl} > \\text{C}-\\text{Br}\\)"}]$op65$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex65$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is A because the bond-dipole magnitude of a two-atom bond scales with the electronegativity difference \(\Delta\text{EN}\) between the bonded atoms, and the given values yield \(\Delta\text{EN}\) of \(1.43\) for \(\text{C}-\text{F}\), \(0.61\) for \(\text{C}-\text{Cl}\), \(0.41\) for \(\text{C}-\text{Br}\), and \(0.11\) for \(\text{C}-\text{I}\). In every one of these bonds the halogen is more electronegative than carbon, so each bond is polarized as \(\text{C}^{\delta+}-\text{X}^{\delta-}\); the larger the \(\Delta\text{EN}\), the greater the partial-charge separation and thus the larger the bond dipole. Because \(1.43 > 0.61 > 0.41 > 0.11\), the dipole magnitudes decrease in the order \(\text{C}-\text{F} > \text{C}-\text{Cl} > \text{C}-\text{Br} > \text{C}-\text{I}\), matching the decreasing electronegativity of the halogen down the group. (Choice B) This reverses the relationship by ordering the bonds from smallest to largest \(\Delta\text{EN}\); iodine has the electronegativity closest to carbon, so \(\text{C}-\text{I}\) is the least polar bond, not the most. (Choice C) Placing \(\text{C}-\text{Cl}\) above \(\text{C}-\text{F}\) ignores that fluorine has the largest electronegativity (\(3.98\)) and therefore the largest \(\Delta\text{EN}\) with carbon; chlorine cannot produce a larger bond dipole than fluorine. (Choice D) Inserting \(\text{C}-\text{I}\) into second place conflates the long \(\text{C}-\text{I}\) bond length with a large dipole, but bond-dipole magnitude here is governed by the small \(\Delta\text{EN}\) of \(0.11\), making \(\text{C}-\text{I}\) the smallest, not the second-largest, dipole. Skill 4$ex65$,
    'hard', '5B', $cc65$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc65$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
