-- Q22 [Bond Polarity & Dipole Moment · hard · Skill 4 · general-chemistry · ans B] (g66)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Bond Polarity & Dipole Moment',
    $qt66$A reference card lists electronegativity-difference values for four covalent bonds: \(\text{S}-\text{H}\) at \(\Delta\text{EN} = 0.38\), \(\text{C}-\text{F}\) at \(\Delta\text{EN} = 1.43\), \(\text{Si}-\text{O}\) at \(\Delta\text{EN} = 1.54\), and \(\text{H}-\text{F}\) at \(\Delta\text{EN} = 1.78\). Using only these tabulated values, the bond that most likely carries the greatest partial charge separation is$qt66$,
    $op66$[{"label":"A","text":"the \\(\\text{C}-\\text{F}\\) bond, because fluorine is the most electronegative element listed."},{"label":"B","text":"the \\(\\text{H}-\\text{F}\\) bond, whose \\(\\Delta\\text{EN} = 1.78\\) is the largest tabulated value."},{"label":"C","text":"the \\(\\text{Si}-\\text{O}\\) bond, whose \\(\\Delta\\text{EN} = 1.54\\) is the second-largest tabulated value."},{"label":"D","text":"the \\(\\text{S}-\\text{H}\\) bond, whose \\(\\Delta\\text{EN} = 0.38\\) is the smallest tabulated value."}]$op66$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex66$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is B because the partial charge separation of a covalent bond grows with the electronegativity difference between the bonded atoms, and \(\text{H}-\text{F}\) carries the largest tabulated \(\Delta\text{EN}\) of \(1.78\). A larger \(\Delta\text{EN}\) means the more electronegative atom pulls the shared electron pair more strongly toward itself, building up a larger partial negative charge (\(\delta^-\)) on that atom and a matching partial positive charge (\(\delta^+\)) on its partner; this unequal sharing is precisely what a more polar bond, with greater partial ionic character, expresses. Among the four values given, the ranking is \(\text{H}-\text{F}\,(1.78) > \text{Si}-\text{O}\,(1.54) > \text{C}-\text{F}\,(1.43) > \text{S}-\text{H}\,(0.38)\), so the bond with the maximum \(\Delta\text{EN}\) is \(\text{H}-\text{F}\). (Choice A) Partial charge separation is set by the difference in electronegativity between the two bonded atoms, not by the absolute electronegativity of either atom alone; although fluorine is the most electronegative element listed, the \(\text{C}-\text{F}\) value of \(1.43\) is smaller than \(\Delta\text{EN}\) for \(\text{H}-\text{F}\), so the presence of fluorine does not by itself maximize the charge separation. (Choice C) This correctly identifies \(\Delta\text{EN}\) as the governing quantity but selects the second-largest tabulated value, \(1.54\) for \(\text{Si}-\text{O}\), rather than the maximum; greatest partial charge separation tracks the single largest \(\Delta\text{EN}\), which is the \(1.78\) of \(\text{H}-\text{F}\). (Choice D) The smallest \(\Delta\text{EN}\) corresponds to the most nearly equal sharing and therefore the least partial charge separation; treating the smallest value, \(0.38\) for \(\text{S}-\text{H}\), as the most polar inverts the relationship between \(\Delta\text{EN}\) and bond polarity. Skill 4$ex66$,
    'hard', '5B', $cc66$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc66$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
